---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import StorySteps from '../../components/StorySteps.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/charts/Figure.astro';
import CompareBars from '../../components/charts/CompareBars.astro';
import BarChart from '../../components/charts/BarChart.astro';
import StatBand from '../../components/charts/StatBand.astro';

const steps = [
  {
    kicker: 'The problem',
    headline: 'A third of bookings never happened',
    body:
      'Show rate sat near 68% — and outcomes were not being recorded consistently, so the true number and its causes were invisible. A booking that no-shows is wasted twice: the ad spend that won the lead, and the counsellor slot that sat empty.',
  },
  {
    kicker: 'What I did',
    headline: 'Made it measurable, then fixed the leak',
    body:
      'First the measurement: every consultation outcome recorded, so show rate was finally trustworthy. Then the analysis — where the drop happened, by channel and by the gap between booking and appointment. That pointed at three targeted fixes: automated reminders, a confirmation step, and same-day follow-ups on the bookings most likely to slip.',
  },
  {
    kicker: 'The result',
    headline: '68% → 82% show rate',
    body:
      'Roughly one in five no-shows recovered — more consultations attended every week from the same bookings, with zero extra ad spend. That is the leverage of analytics: I did not spend a rupee more on leads, I just made the leak visible and closed it.',
  },
];

const fixes = [
  {
    title: 'Reliable outcome tracking',
    desc: 'Every consultation outcome recorded in GoHighLevel, so show rate could be measured and trusted before anyone tried to move it.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Reminders + a confirmation step',
    desc: 'Automated nudges and an explicit confirm action before each consultation, so intent was re-tested closer to the appointment.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Same-day follow-ups',
    desc: 'Fast follow-up routed to the bookings the analysis flagged as most likely to slip — long booking-to-appointment gaps and lower-intent channels.',
  },
];
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="The half that never showed up"
  eyebrow="Impact story · Analytics → Revenue"
  hook="How fixing what we could see lifted the show rate — same leads, same spend, more consultations attended."
  description="Show rate rose from 68% to 82% by fixing outcome tracking first, then closing the drop-off it revealed. No additional ad spend."
  org="The Migration"
  year="2026"
  stack={['Funnel Analytics', 'Data Governance', 'GoHighLevel', 'Attribution', 'Python']}
>
  <p class="lead">
    We were paying to book consultations — and quietly, nearly a third of them never
    happened.
  </p>

  <StorySteps steps={steps} />

  <Figure
    title="Show rate, before and after"
    sub="Out of every 100 booked consultations"
  >
    <CompareBars
      states={[
        { label: 'Before', value: 68 },
        { label: 'After', value: 82, after: true },
      ]}
      positiveLabel="Attended"
      negativeLabel="No-show"
    />
  </Figure>

  <StatBand
    stats={[
      { value: '+14 pts', label: 'Show rate lift' },
      { value: '~1 in 5', label: 'No-shows recovered' },
      { value: '$0', label: 'Extra ad spend' },
      { value: 'Same', label: 'Leads and bookings' },
    ]}
  />

  <h2>Where the drop-off was hiding</h2>
  <p>
    Once outcomes were recorded reliably, the pattern was not uniform. Two variables
    explained most of the variance: the acquisition channel a booking came from, and
    how many days sat between the booking and the appointment itself.
  </p>

  <Figure
    title="Show rate by booking-to-appointment gap"
    sub="The longer the wait, the more intent decays"
    note="Illustrative shape based on the pattern the analysis surfaced; exact figures are commercially sensitive."
  >
    <BarChart
      rows={[
        { label: 'Same day', value: 89, display: '89%' },
        { label: '1–2 days', value: 81, display: '81%' },
        { label: '3–5 days', value: 70, display: '70%' },
        { label: '6+ days', value: 54, display: '54%' },
      ]}
      max={100}
      highlight={['6+ days']}
      categoryHeader="Booking-to-appointment gap"
      valueHeader="Show rate"
    />
  </Figure>

  <p>
    The 6+ day cohort was the one to attack. It was not the largest group, but it was
    where the most attended consultations were being lost per booking — which made it
    the obvious target for the confirmation step and same-day follow-up.
  </p>

  <h2>The three fixes</h2>
  <div class="fix-grid">
    {fixes.map((f) => (
      <div class="card">
        <h3>{f.title}</h3>
        <p>{f.desc}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2>Why this one matters</h2>
  <p>
    Every marketing team has a version of this problem: a number everyone quotes that
    nobody can actually trust. The instinct is to go straight to fixing the number.
    The order that works is the other one — make it measurable, let the data tell you
    where the loss concentrates, then spend your effort there.
  </p>
  <p>
    It also cost nothing. No additional media budget, no new headcount, no new tool.
    The same leads and the same bookings produced meaningfully more attended
    consultations, which is exactly where analytics earns its keep.
  </p>
</CaseStudyLayout>

<style>
  .fix-grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1rem;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 15rem), 1fr));
    margin-block: 1.8rem 2.5rem;
  }
  .fix-grid h3 { font-size: 1rem; }
  .fix-grid p { font-size: .9rem; }
</style>
